{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And one who curses his father and his mother.</b> Why is this said? Since it says ״אִישׁ אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יְקַלֵּל אֶת אָבִיו״ (Leviticus 20), I have only a man who cursed his father — from where do I derive a woman who cursed her father? Scripture says ״וּמְקַלֵּל אָבִיו וְאִמּוֹ״ unstipulated — whether a man or whether a woman. If so, why is ״אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר יְקַלֵּל״ said? To exclude the minor (Mekhilta)." "<b>He shall surely be put to death.</b> By stoning. And every place where ״דָּמָיו בוֹ״ is said — it is by stoning, and it is a model-case for all of them: ״בָּאֶבֶן יִרְגְּמוּ אֹתָם דְּמֵיהֶם בָּם״ (Leviticus 20). And concerning one who curses his father and his mother, ״דָּמָיו בוֹ״ is said."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:37.132506399Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}